      note: "The 29 July meeting resolved as a hold at 3.50–3.75%, but with a hawkish 9-3 vote split (three dissents for a hike). CME FedWatch-implied odds of a hike at the 16 September meeting held near ~81% through 30 July, collapsed to roughly 44% (56% hold) by 7 August after nonfarm payrolls unexpectedly fell 23,000 in July, drifted back up toward roughly even (50/50) by 11 August (CNN), then eased to roughly 38–42% hike / 58–62% hold on 12 August as July CPI printed in line with consensus, and to roughly 35% hike / 65% hold on 13 August after July PPI undershot. Odds firmed back toward roughly even around mid-week on the 30-year's 19-year-high spike and the lapsed US-Iran ceasefire MoU, eased to roughly 31–35% hike by 18 August on a softer data run, then firmed again to roughly 40% hike / 60% hold on 26 August as July core PCE held at 3.3% y/y (in line) but headline ran hot at 3.7% y/y with spending flat — CME's own tool showed 40.1% hike / 59.9% hold that day. Chair Warsh's 28 August Jackson Hole keynote then tilted odds sharply more hawkish through the session: intraday CME FedWatch-implied odds rose to roughly 46-50% hike, and by the close 30-day fed-funds futures pricing implied 57.5% odds of a hike (up from ~35.5% the day before), per CNBC — the highest reading of the cycle. Odds firmed further into Monday 31 August: fed-funds futures priced a fresh-cycle-high 60.4% hike probability by the morning (from ~56% Friday) after the weekend's US strike on Iran's Larak Island and Iran's retaliatory strikes on US bases in Jordan and the UAE revived the oil-driven inflation risk (Brent +2.9% to ~$90.70/bbl), then ticked up again to roughly 62% by midday as US equities extended their losses and BoE Governor Bailey's FSB letter warning of AI-bubble/market-correction risk added to the cautious tone; prediction markets showed a closer call (Kalshi ~48%, Polymarket ~49%). Deutsche Bank said it now expects hikes at both the September and December meetings; Wall Street stays split: BofA, PGIM and now Deutsche Bank expect hikes at the remaining 2026 meetings, while Barclays, Jefferies, Morgan Stanley, Truist and UBS see the Fed on hold. Sources: Federal Reserve (28 Aug speech transcript); CNBC/CME FedWatch (28 & 31 Aug); Benzinga (31 Aug); Yahoo Finance (31 Aug midday); Reuters/CNBC (18 Aug dollar); CNBC (13 Aug PPI); CNN (11 Aug).",
