    bottomLine: "The MPC held at 3.75% on 30 July but the vote split hardened to 6–3 (Greene, Mann and Pill dissenting for an immediate 25bp hike) — one more dissenter than June's 7–2 and a narrower hold than the 7–2 most economists expected. The Bank's own projections show CPI peaking near 3.2% in Q4 2026; markets continue to price a 25bp hike arriving around the November or December MPC meeting, taking Bank Rate toward ~4.20% by end-2026 and a ~4.20–4.25% peak in early 2027, though Berenberg and Oxford Economics see that as an overshoot and expect a hold well into 2027. The FTSE 100 touched a fresh intraday record (10,979.60) on the decision day but closed down 11.14 points (-0.1%) at 10,897.27 as early gains faded after Bailey's press conference, even with Rolls-Royce up 6.0% on strong earnings; Rentokil Initial slumped 21% on weak North American demand, and sterling eased slightly off its intraday high to trade near $1.338. Burnham/Healey's VAT cut on household electricity (from October) trims CPI by an estimated 0.10pp, a small disinflationary offset alongside their earlier 20% business-rates cut for pubs, clubs and music venues from April 2027. UK data on 18 August showed employers still shedding staff (payrolled employment -13,000 in July, vacancies at a five-year low of 707,000) while the 30-year gilt yield was swept up in the global long-end bond selloff toward 5.83–5.84%, near its highs for the year — a soft-labour/high-yield combination that reinforces the case for a hold at the 17 September MPC meeting even as fiscal pressure builds ahead of the 28 October Budget. A Reuters poll published 18 August found most economists expect a hold through year-end (a narrow majority still see a cut by mid-2027), as the 19 August July CPI print landed at 2.9% y/y as expected (from 2.6%) with a hotter core reading (2.6% vs 2.5% forecast) on the Ofgem energy-cap rise, drawing only a muted market reaction, even as a fresh overnight Asia chip/bond selloff (Kospi -6.8%, Nikkei -3.1%, Japan's 10-year yield at its highest since 1996) keeps global-yield risk live. Friday's flash PMI strengthened modestly (Services 52.8, a 6-month high, from 52.1; Composite 52.5, a 4-month high, from 52.2; Manufacturing slipped to a 5-month low of 51.5) even as July retail sales fell 0.5% m/m, annual growth slowed to 1.6% from 3.8%, and ONS data showed a surprise £1.8bn July public-sector deficit (vs an expected balance, and over £2bn above the OBR's own forecast) — a fresh complication for Chancellor Healey's 28 October Budget — with the FTSE 100 edging higher on a gold/copper-led mining rally as the 10-year gilt yield held near 5.05%. The FTSE 100 extended its advance into Monday 24 August, closing up 0.4% at 10,854.32 (Airtel Africa, Weir Group and InterContinental Hotels leading) as falling oil and easing bond yields offset the newly-detailed Iran-sanctions overhang, while sterling held near a six-month high above $1.36 on continued dollar weakness from the Treasury's expanded long-bond buyback programme. Ofgem confirmed on 26 August that the October–December price cap will rise only 4% (£60/yr, to £1,723) — far below the ~13% rise feared in July — a modest positive for the MPC's near-term inflation read, though markets showed little reaction on the day, with focus on the same day's US PCE print and Friday's Jackson Hole keynote.",
